1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:11,662
It's January in Norway, around the year nine hundred. The sun barely clears the mountains before it sets again. It's freezing. And Tom has months of winter still ahead of him.

2
00:00:11,942 --> 00:00:21,201
No heating. No supermarket. No electric light. Just a wooden house, a fire, and whatever food he managed to store before the snow came.

3
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:30,889
And in the far north of Norway it's even worse. At the peak of the polar night, the sun doesn't rise at all. Not for a few hours. For whole days.

4
00:00:31,169 --> 00:00:43,819
So how did Vikings actually survive winter? By the end of this video, you'll know what they ate, how they stayed warm, what they did all day in the dark, and a Viking winter word you probably say every single year.

5
00:00:44,099 --> 00:00:48,643
First, to a Viking, winter wasn't just a season. It was half of the year.

6
00:00:48,923 --> 00:01:02,214
The Old Norse year had only two seasons. Summer and winter. And when Vikings counted years, they usually counted winters. A Viking wouldn't say he was twenty years old. He'd say he had lived twenty winters.

7
00:01:02,494 --> 00:01:10,025
Winter began in late October, with a festival called the winter nights. And the month that followed had a brutal job.

8
00:01:10,305 --> 00:01:18,817
In the old Icelandic calendar, its name refers to the half-digested contents of animal guts. Because this was slaughter month.

9
00:01:19,097 --> 00:01:27,075
Here's why. Every animal that stayed alive all winter had to be fed with hay. And hay was the most precious thing on the farm.

10
00:01:27,355 --> 00:01:35,846
Hayfields were walled in and kept close to the house. If the hay ran out before spring, the animals starved. And then, so could you.

11
00:01:36,126 --> 00:01:45,769
So in autumn, farmers made a hard choice. Keep the best animals to breed and milk. Slaughter the rest, and turn them into food for the dark months.

12
00:01:46,049 --> 00:01:52,641
Now, let's step inside Tom's house. Because the Viking longhouse was basically a survival machine.

13
00:01:52,921 --> 00:02:06,190
A typical longhouse was five to seven meters wide and anywhere from fifteen to seventy-five meters long. The reconstructed chieftain's house at the Lofotr Viking Museum is over eighty meters long.

14
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:15,451
Where timber was scarce, like in Iceland, the walls were built from thick blocks of turf. That's basically insulation you could cut out of the ground.

15
00:02:15,731 --> 00:02:24,542
Down the middle of the house burned a long hearth. That one fire did everything. It cooked the food, heated the room, and gave most of the light.

16
00:02:24,822 --> 00:02:35,852
There was no chimney. The smoke just drifted up and out through a hole in the roof. So in winter, the inside of a longhouse was warm, dark, and very, very smoky.

17
00:02:36,132 --> 00:02:38,777
And here's a twist. Tom wasn't alone in there.

18
00:02:39,057 --> 00:02:50,385
In many longhouses, one end was reserved for the animals in winter. Cows, sheep, all under the same roof as the family. Their body heat helped keep everyone warm.

19
00:02:50,665 --> 00:02:57,918
Families slept on wide wooden benches that ran along the walls, close together. Privacy? Not really a thing.

20
00:02:58,198 --> 00:03:09,526
And for light after dark, they burned oil lamps, little pots with a wick, filled with cod liver oil, or seal or whale oil. Candles were too expensive for most families.

21
00:03:09,806 --> 00:03:16,590
Quick thing. If you're enjoying Tom's trip through history, hit subscribe. It's free, and it keeps Tom warm.

22
00:03:16,870 --> 00:03:20,454
So, what did a Viking eat when the fields were buried under snow?

23
00:03:20,734 --> 00:03:29,054
Lots of fish. Herring and salmon were dried, or packed in salt. And in the far north, there was the ultimate winter food: stockfish.

24
00:03:29,334 --> 00:03:41,089
Stockfish is cod, hung on wooden racks in the freezing, dry air of northern Norway until all the water is gone. One historian compared it to beef jerky, only fishy.

25
00:03:41,369 --> 00:03:47,555
And it lasted. Some medieval accounts say stockfish was still edible ten years after it was dried.

26
00:03:47,835 --> 00:04:01,873
It travelled, too. DNA from cod bones found at Haithabu, a Viking trading town in what's now northern Germany, shows the fish came from the Arctic waters off Lofoten. More than a thousand miles away.

27
00:04:02,153 --> 00:04:11,347
Then there was dairy. Butter, cheese, buttermilk, and in Iceland, skyr, a thick, sour, yogurt-like cheese that Icelanders still eat today.

28
00:04:11,627 --> 00:04:21,036
Sheep's milk mattered more than you'd think. It's rich in vitamin C, which helped people fight scurvy in the dark months, when there was nothing fresh to eat.

29
00:04:21,315 --> 00:04:35,267
Where you lived changed the menu. In Greenland, archaeologists found that poorer Viking farms ate a lot more seal, while richer farms ate more caribou. Winter food was also a sign of how rich you were.

30
00:04:35,548 --> 00:04:46,235
And no, it wasn't all bland. Cooks seasoned with onions, garlic, dill and coriander. Though, sadly, not a single original Viking recipe has survived.

31
00:04:46,516 --> 00:04:57,374
By midwinter, the old Icelandic calendar even had a month whose name is about sucking fat. In the darkest weeks, families lived off stored fat and bone marrow.

32
00:04:57,654 --> 00:05:02,305
But Vikings didn't just hide indoors. Snow and ice were also a highway.

33
00:05:02,585 --> 00:05:12,078
Archaeologists have found large numbers of Viking Age ice skates, made from the foot bones of horses and cattle, polished flat on the bottom.

34
00:05:12,358 --> 00:05:23,238
You didn't lift your feet. You stood on the bones and pushed yourself along with a pole tipped with an iron spike. Kids used them. Adults used them. It was transport and fun.

35
00:05:23,518 --> 00:05:37,534
And then, there were skis. In the mountains of southern Norway, melting ice gave up a pair of wooden skis about thirteen hundred years old, from just before the Viking Age. Still with their leather and birch-bark bindings.

36
00:05:37,814 --> 00:05:47,713
Skiing was so important that the Norse had a god for it. Ullr, the god of snow, hunting and the bow. Snorri Sturluson called him the ski-god.

37
00:05:47,993 --> 00:05:54,947
By the way, if you've learned something already, a like really helps Tom get out of the snow and onto more screens.

38
00:05:55,228 --> 00:06:01,265
So what did Vikings do with all those long, dark days? A lot of work, and a lot of games.

39
00:06:01,545 --> 00:06:08,329
Winter was the time to spin wool, weave cloth, mend tools, and repair everything that broke over the summer.

40
00:06:08,609 --> 00:06:20,065
And they played board games. One of them was hnefatafl, the king's table. One side protects a king and tries to sneak him off the board. The other side tries to trap him.

41
00:06:20,345 --> 00:06:32,782
Games could get heated. One saga tells of a stepmother who threw a game piece at a player and knocked out his eye. Another feast in Denmark ended with a man flipping the board, and later being killed.

42
00:06:33,062 --> 00:06:45,500
Even Viking raiders played. In the winter of eight seventy-two, the Great Viking Army camped at Torksey in England. Archaeologists there have found more than three hundred lead gaming pieces.

43
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:54,740
Those warriors didn't just play, either. They melted down silver, made tools, sewed and fished in the river. A winter camp was a whole town.

44
00:06:55,020 --> 00:07:06,390
And then there were the stories. A verse in the Hávamál, the sayings of Odin, puts the winter rule simply: Fire he needs who with frozen knees has come from the cold without.

45
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:10,681
But the biggest moment of the whole winter came right in the middle of it.

46
00:07:10,961 --> 00:07:20,284
Yule. A pagan festival that began around the winter solstice, when the sun was at its weakest, and lasted for several nights of feasting and drinking.

47
00:07:20,564 --> 00:07:30,420
And the drinking was serious. According to a saga about King Hakon the Good, every free man had to drink about four gallons of booze over three nights.

48
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:42,582
Yule was also when Vikings swore solemn oaths, kind of like New Year's resolutions. Except breaking one was a very big deal, and some were sworn with a hand on a sacrificed boar.

49
00:07:42,862 --> 00:07:52,633
And Yule was a time to remember the dead. That's why, even today, many Scandinavians light candles in churchyards in the darkest weeks of the year.

50
00:07:52,913 --> 00:07:57,137
Okay. The Viking winter word I promised you. Actually, there are two.

51
00:07:57,417 --> 00:08:06,761
The first is Yule itself. People in the English-speaking world still sing about Yuletide and burn a Yule log, more than a thousand years later.

52
00:08:07,041 --> 00:08:19,905
The second one you'll say every time it snows. In Old Norse, a long snowshoe was called a skíð, which literally meant a stick of wood. English borrowed it from Norwegian in the eighteen-eighties. Ski.

53
00:08:20,185 --> 00:08:32,729
So, how did Vikings survive winter? Hay for the animals, salt and stockfish for the family, cows for central heating, bone skates for the commute, and one giant party in the darkest week of the year.

54
00:08:33,009 --> 00:08:44,337
Could you survive a winter in a smoky longhouse with a cow for a roommate? Tell us in the comments. And next time on Back Then, Tom goes to work on the pyramids of Egypt.

